# kybernet/service_sandbox.cyr — per-service sandbox applied at pre_exec
# Requires: privdrop.cyr, sandbox.cyr, seccomp.cyr, argonaut types
#
# This is the payload of the 2026-08-24 audit's HIGH-1. seccomp, Landlock
# and capability drops can only be installed between fork and exec, argonaut
# owns that window, and until 1.9.0 it exposed no seam — so kybernet's 686
# lines of security code were reachable from nothing. argonaut 1.9.0 added
# `argonaut_set_pre_exec_hook`; this is the hook.
#
# It runs IN THE CHILD, after fork, after the signal-mask reset, immediately
# before execve. Returning non-zero aborts the child with exit 126 and the
# service never runs — the sandbox failing is not a reason to launch the
# service unconfined.
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING:
#   1. no_new_privs   — precondition for both Landlock and seccomp, and it
#                       is what stops a setuid binary handing the privileges
#                       back on exec.
#   2. capabilities   — bounding set + the live effective/permitted sets.
#   3. Landlock       — filesystem confinement.
#   4. seccomp        — LAST. An installed filter would otherwise kill the
#                       prctl/capset/landlock calls above unless every one
#                       of them were allowlisted.
#
# POLICY IS OPT-IN PER SERVICE. argonaut's ServiceDefinition carries
# `seccomp` / `landlock` / `capabilities`, all 0 by default, and argonaut
# treats them as opaque — kybernet defines them as agnostik
# seccomp_profile / sandbox_config / capability_set pointers. A service
# with no policy gets a no-op hook and behaves exactly as before 1.4.3.
# That is deliberate: a uniform allowlist applied to every service is an
# excellent way to make a system unbootable, and PID 1 is the wrong place
# to discover that. Wiring the default AGNOS services to real profiles is
# the 1.5.x arc.
fn kyb_pre_exec(svc_def) {
    if (svc_def == 0) { return 0; }

    var caps = svc_def_capabilities(svc_def);
    var ll   = svc_def_landlock(svc_def);
    var sc   = svc_def_seccomp(svc_def);

    # No policy at all — nothing to enforce, and no_new_privs is not
    # imposed on services that never asked to be confined.
    if (caps == 0 && ll == 0 && sc == 0) { return 0; }

    # 1. no_new_privs. Defaults on for any confined service (both Landlock
    #    and seccomp require it, and it is what stops a setuid binary
    #    handing the privileges straight back on exec); a policy may opt
    #    out explicitly. 1.5.2.
    var want_nnp = 1;
    if (sc != 0) { want_nnp = kyb_policy_no_new_privs(sc); }
    if (want_nnp == 1) {
        if (is_err_result(set_no_new_privs()) == 1) { return 1; }
    }

    # 2. capabilities
    if (caps != 0) {
        if (is_err_result(drop_caps_from_set(caps)) == 1) { return 1; }
    }

    # 3. Landlock
    if (ll != 0) {
        if (is_err_result(sandbox_from_config(ll)) == 1) { return 1; }
    }

    # 4. seccomp — last. `sc` is kybernet's KybSeccompPolicy (argonaut
    #    treats the field as opaque), naming a profile rather than carrying
    #    a raw syscall list: a filter is not something to hand-assemble in
    #    a config file, and a wrong one is a service that dies on its first
    #    read().
    if (sc != 0) {
        if (kyb_policy_kind(sc) == SECCOMP_BASIC) {
            if (is_err_result(seccomp_apply(seccomp_basic_service())) == 1) { return 1; }
        }
    }

    return 0;
}

